{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T05:29:00Z","timestamp":1790054940326,"version":"4.0.1"},"reference-count":65,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"S2","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2014,9,23]],"date-time":"2014-09-23T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1411430400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["J of Money Credit Banking"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2014,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Unemployment and inflation lower well\u2010being. The macroeconomist Arthur Okun characterized the negative effects of unemployment and inflation by the <jats:italic>misery index\u2014<\/jats:italic>the sum of the unemployment and inflation rates. This paper makes use of a large European data set, covering the period 1975\u20132013, to estimate happiness equations in which an individual subjective measure of life satisfaction is regressed against unemployment and inflation rate (controlling for personal characteristics, country, and year fixed effects). We find, conventionally, that both higher unemployment and higher inflation lower well\u2010being. We also discover that unemployment depresses well\u2010being more than inflation. We characterize this well\u2010being trade\u2010off between unemployment and inflation using what we describe as the misery <jats:italic>ratio<\/jats:italic>. Our estimates with European data imply that a 1 percentage point increase in the unemployment rate lowers well\u2010being by more than five times as much as a 1 percentage point increase in the inflation rate.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111\/jmcb.12154","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2014,9,24]],"date-time":"2014-09-24T03:14:47Z","timestamp":1411528487000},"page":"117-141","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":98,"title":["The Happiness Trade\u2010Off between Unemployment and Inflation"],"prefix":"10.1111","volume":"46","author":[{"given":"DAVID G.","family":"BLANCHFLOWER","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"DAVID N.F.","family":"BELL","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"ALBERTO","family":"MONTAGNOLI","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"MIRKO","family":"MORO","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2014,9,23]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_8_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jpubeco.2003.07.006"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1086\/257766"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_4_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/0304-3932(76)90002-7"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1093\/oxrep\/grr011"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_6_1","first-page":"11","article-title":"Youth Unemployment in Europe and the United States","volume":"1","author":"Bell David N.F.","year":"2011","journal-title":"Nordic Economic Policy Review"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2307\/2297813"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2307\/2233554"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_9_1","volume-title":"Measuring the Subjective Well\u2010Being of Nations: National Accounts of Time\u2010Use and Well\u2010Being","author":"Blanchflower David G.","year":"2007"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_10_1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"Blanchflower David G.(2007b) \u201cIs Unemployment More Costly Than Inflation?\u201dNBER Working Paper No. W13505.","DOI":"10.3386\/w13505"},{"key":"e_1_2_8_11_1","unstructured":"Blanchflower David G. NicholasChristakis andAndrew J.Oswald(2011) \u201cAn Introduction to the Structure of Biomarker Equations.\u201d Mimeo."},{"key":"